„­bible_version£KJV¤book'§chapter¨sections›…¤book'§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙTake heed that ye do not your alms before men, to be seen of them: otherwise ye have no reward of your Father which is in heaven.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÔTherefore when thou doest thine alms, do not sound a trumpet before thee, as the hypocrites do in the synagogues and in the streets, that they may have glory of men. Verily I say unto you, They have their reward.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙOBut when thou doest alms, let not thy left hand know what thy right hand doeth:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙhthat thine alms may be in secret: and thy Father which seeth in secret himself shall reward thee openly.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book'§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙàAnd when thou prayest, thou shalt not be as the hypocrites are: for they love to pray standing in the synagogues and in the corners of the streets, that they may be seen of men. Verily I say unto you, They have their reward.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ»But thou, when thou prayest, enter into thy closet, and when thou hast shut thy door, pray to thy Father which is in secret; and thy Father which seeth in secret shall reward thee openly.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙBut when ye pray, use not vain repetitions, as the heathen do: for they think that they shall be heard for their much speaking.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙkBe not ye therefore like unto them: for your Father knoweth what things ye have need of, before ye ask him.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book'§chapter«first_verse	ªlast_verse¦verses•„¥verse	¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙZAfter this manner therefore pray ye: Our Father which art in heaven, Hallowed be thy name.¦layout ªlast_verse	„¥verse
¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ@Thy kingdom come. Thy will be done in earth, as it is in heaven.¦layout ªlast_verse
„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ!Give us this day our daily bread.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ4And forgive us our debts, as we forgive our debtors.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‚And lead us not into temptation, but deliver us from evil: For thine is the kingdom, and the power, and the glory, for ever. Amen.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book'§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses’„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙSFor if ye forgive men their trespasses, your heavenly Father will also forgive you:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ]but if ye forgive not men their trespasses, neither will your Father forgive your trespasses.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book'§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ½Moreover when ye fast, be not, as the hypocrites, of a sad countenance: for they disfigure their faces, that they may appear unto men to fast. Verily I say unto you, They have their reward.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙBBut thou, when thou fastest, anoint thine head, and wash thy face;¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙthat thou appear not unto men to fast, but unto thy Father which is in secret: and thy Father, which seeth in secret, shall reward thee openly.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book'§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ|Lay not up for yourselves treasures upon earth, where moth and rust doth corrupt, and where thieves break through and steal:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙŠbut lay up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where neither moth nor rust doth corrupt, and where thieves do not break through nor steal:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ:for where your treasure is, there will your heart be also.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book'§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses’„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙjThe light of the body is the eye: if therefore thine eye be single, thy whole body shall be full of light.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ“But if thine eye be evil, thy whole body shall be full of darkness. If therefore the light that is in thee be darkness, how great is that darkness!¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book'§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses‘„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙªNo man can serve two masters: for either he will hate the one, and love the other; or else he will hold to the one, and despise the other. Ye cannot serve God and mammon.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book'§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙËTherefore I say unto you, Take no thought for your life, what ye shall eat, or what ye shall drink; nor yet for your body, what ye shall put on. Is not the life more than meat, and the body than raiment?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¤Behold the fowls of the air: for they sow not, neither do they reap, nor gather into barns; yet your heavenly Father feedeth them. Are ye not much better than they?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙBWhich of you by taking thought can add one cubit unto his stature?¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book'§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙzAnd why take ye thought for raiment? Consider the lilies of the field, how they grow; they toil not, neither do they spin:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ]and yet I say unto you, That even Solomon in all his glory was not arrayed like one of these.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¢Wherefore, if God so clothe the grass of the field, which to day is, and to morrow is cast into the oven, shall he not much more clothe you, O ye of little faith?¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book'§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse"¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙsTherefore take no thought, saying, What shall we eat? or, What shall we drink? or, Wherewithal shall we be clothed?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse ¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙz(For after all these things do the Gentiles seek:) for your heavenly Father knoweth that ye have need of all these things.¦layout ªlast_verse „¥verse!¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙjBut seek ye first the kingdom of God, and his righteousness; and all these things shall be added unto you.¦layout ªlast_verse!„¥verse"¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ’Take therefore no thought for the morrow: for the morrow shall take thought for the things of itself. Sufficient unto the day is the evil thereof.¦layout ªlast_verse"